# React Todos App - Cursor Rules

## Project Overview

This is a React Todos application built with TypeScript, Vite, and modern styling solutions. The app integrates with the JSONPlaceholder API to provide a full-featured todo management system.

## Tech Stack & Architecture

### Core Technologies

- **React 19** with TypeScript for type safety
- **Vite** for fast development and building
- **Stylus** for global app styling
- **Styled Components** for component-level styling
- **Vitest** with jsdom for unit testing
- **ESLint** and **Prettier** for code quality

### State Management

- **NO Redux** - Use React's built-in state management (useState, useReducer, Context API)
- Prefer local component state when possible
- Use React Context for shared state across components
- Implement custom hooks for complex state logic (e.g., useTodos)
- Handle async operations with proper loading and error states

## File Structure & Organization

### Directory Structure

```
src/
├── components/          # Reusable UI components
│   ├── Button.tsx      # Styled button component with variants
│   ├── Input.tsx       # Form input component with validation
│   └── TodoItem.tsx    # Individual todo item with CRUD operations
├── pages/              # Page-level components
│   └── TodoPage.tsx    # Main todos page with filtering and management
├── hooks/              # Custom React hooks
│   └── useTodos.ts     # Todo state management and API integration
├── services/           # API service layer
│   └── todoService.ts  # JSONPlaceholder API integration
├── types/              # TypeScript type definitions
│   └── todo.ts         # Todo-related interfaces and types
├── styles/             # Global Stylus styles
│   └── global.styl     # App-wide styling and design tokens
├── __tests__/          # All test files
│   ├── setup.ts        # Test environment configuration
│   └── App.test.tsx    # Component tests
└── assets/             # Static assets
```

### Component Structure

- Use functional components with hooks
- Implement TypeScript interfaces for props
- Use styled-components for component-specific styling
- Place components in appropriate directories based on scope
- Follow the established component hierarchy: App → TodoPage → TodoItem
- Use type-only imports for TypeScript interfaces (verbatimModuleSyntax enabled)

## Styling Guidelines

### Global Styling (Stylus)

- Use `src/styles/` directory for global Stylus files
- Import global styles in `main.tsx` or `App.tsx`
- Follow BEM methodology for class naming
- Use CSS custom properties for theming
- Implement modern CSS reset for cross-browser consistency
- Use `:focus-visible` for accessibility-focused styling

### Component Styling (Styled Components)

- Create styled components within component files
- Use TypeScript for styled-component props
- Follow naming convention: `StyledComponentName`
- Use theme provider for consistent design tokens
- Add `font-family: inherit` to form elements for consistency
- Use `:focus-visible` instead of `:focus` for better accessibility
- Implement proper disabled states for interactive elements

## Testing Strategy

### Test Organization

- All tests go in `src/__tests__/` directory
- Mirror the source directory structure in tests
- Use descriptive test file names: `ComponentName.test.tsx`

### Testing Guidelines

- Use Vitest with jsdom for DOM testing
- Write unit tests for all components
- Test user interactions and state changes
- Use React Testing Library for component testing
- Mock external dependencies appropriately

## Code Quality

### TypeScript

- Enable strict mode
- Define interfaces for all props and state
- Use type inference where appropriate
- Avoid `any` type - use proper typing

### ESLint & Prettier

- Use provided ESLint configuration
- Format code with Prettier
- Follow consistent naming conventions
- Use meaningful variable and function names

## Development Workflow

### Component Development

1. Create TypeScript interface for props
2. Implement component logic
3. Add styled-components for styling
4. Write unit tests
5. Update documentation if needed

### State Management

1. Start with local component state
2. Use Context API for shared state
3. Implement custom hooks for complex logic
4. Avoid prop drilling

## File Naming Conventions

- Components: PascalCase (`TodoItem.tsx`)
- Hooks: camelCase with `use` prefix (`useTodos.ts`)
- Utilities: camelCase (`formatDate.ts`)
- Types: PascalCase (`TodoTypes.ts`)
- Test files: `.test.tsx` or `.spec.tsx`

## Import/Export Guidelines

- Use named exports for components
- Use default exports sparingly
- Group imports: React, third-party, local
- Use absolute imports for better maintainability

## Performance Considerations

- Use React.memo for expensive components
- Implement proper dependency arrays in useEffect
- Lazy load components when appropriate
- Optimize re-renders with useCallback and useMemo

## Accessibility

- Use semantic HTML elements
- Implement proper ARIA attributes
- Ensure keyboard navigation
- Test with screen readers

## API Integration

### Service Layer

- Create dedicated service classes for API calls
- Use TypeScript interfaces for request/response types
- Implement proper error handling and loading states
- Follow RESTful conventions for API endpoints
- Use JSONPlaceholder API for development and testing

### Data Flow

- Service layer → Custom hooks → Components
- Implement optimistic updates for better UX
- Handle API errors gracefully with user feedback
- Use proper TypeScript types for all API interactions

## CSS Reset & Styling Best Practices

### Modern CSS Reset
- Implement comprehensive CSS reset in `src/styles/global.styl`
- Remove default browser styles for consistent cross-browser experience
- Use `box-sizing: border-box` for all elements
- Reset form elements (button, input, select, textarea) to remove defaults
- Support `prefers-reduced-motion` for accessibility

### Accessibility Styling
- Use `:focus-visible` for keyboard navigation indicators
- Implement proper focus outlines with `outline-offset`
- Remove focus outlines for mouse users with `:focus:not(:focus-visible)`
- Ensure sufficient color contrast ratios
- Support reduced motion preferences

### Typography System
- Define consistent heading hierarchy (h1-h6)
- Use CSS custom properties for font sizes and line heights
- Implement proper spacing for paragraphs and headings
- Ensure readable line lengths and spacing

## Error Handling

- Use Error Boundaries for component errors
- Implement proper error states
- Provide meaningful error messages
- Log errors appropriately
- Handle API errors with user-friendly messages
